  "description": "A themed round deals only films carrying its theme tag: the pool's favourite (untagged) and a film carrying a DIFFERENT tag are both ineligible, so the one themed film clearing the approval floor wins outright",
  "spec_clause": "Phase 1 / Eligibility: 'A movie is eligible if status = pool, it carries the round's theme tag when the round has one ..., and: ... Require coverage >= COVERAGE_FLOOR' + Themed rounds: 'In a themed round a film must also carry that tag to be eligible ... a film carrying a different tag is as ineligible as one carrying none.' + Selecting finalists: 'Exactly one clears it -> it wins outright, skip Phase 2.'",
  "rationale": "4 attendees; the round's theme is 'halloween'. Tag membership is checked first, on the normalised tag key, before any floor: m1 (no tags) and m4 (tagged 'christmas' only) do not carry 'halloween' and are ineligible however they were swiped — m1 has 4 rows, 4 yes, coverage 1.00, approval 1.00; m4 the same. m2 (halloween): 4 rows, 3 yes -> coverage 4/4 = 1.00 >= 0.6, approval 3/4 = 0.75 >= 0.5, clears. m3 (halloween): 4 rows, 1 yes -> coverage 1.00, eligible, approval 1/4 = 0.25 < 0.5, does not clear. Exactly one eligible movie clears the approval floor -> m2 wins outright, Phase 2 skipped, so the recorded pair votes are never tallied (interpretations 5 and 6). Two plausible wrong readings give two different documented winners: IGNORING THE THEME makes m1, m4, m2 eligible finalists (4, 4, 3 yes; m3 still fails approval) and the pair votes make m1 the Condorcet winner (4-0 over m2 and over m4); treating ANY TAG as qualifying admits m4 alongside m2 and m3, finalists {m4, m2}, and the pair votes make m4 the Condorcet winner (4-0 over m2). Everything after the eligibility filter is the unthemed rule set unchanged.",
